IAM stands for 𝗜𝗱𝗲𝗻𝘁𝗶𝘁𝘆 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗔𝗰𝗰𝗲𝘀𝘀 𝗠𝗮𝗻𝗮𝗴𝗲𝗺𝗲𝗻𝘁. It is the backbone of secure cloud access in AWS. 

Knowing who can access what is very critical in the cloud environment. 
IAM enforces the principle of protecting sensitive data and to keep everything in control; this ranges from users, to policies, to roles. 

Here are some of the key terms that I have found being used in IAM:

• 𝗨𝘀𝗲𝗿: Someone who has their own identity with long term credentials (login access).

• 𝗚𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗽: A collection of users that have shared permissions.

• 𝗥𝗼𝗹𝗲: A temporary identity with specific permissions, used by services or users. 

• 𝗣𝗼𝗹𝗶𝗰𝘆: A JSON document that defines what actions are allowed/denied on resources.

IAM is crucial in AWS as it helps manage who can access what, when and how. It is a fundamental part of cloud security. 
